  <front>
    <title abbrev="nish/hex">Spec 0001: nish/hex — hexadecimal encoding and decoding</title>
    <seriesInfo name="Internet-Draft" value="draft-hex-00"/>
    <author initials="N." surname="Authors" fullname="Nish Authors">
      <organization>nish</organization>
      <address>
      </address>
    </author>
    <date year="2026" month="November" day="16"/>
    <abstract>
      <?line 15?>

<t>Status: proposed. Module: std/hex.ts (nish/hex). A hexadecimal codec for
nish: hexEncode writes lowercase hex, and hexDecode reads hex in any case and
refuses everything else with null.</t>
    </abstract>
  </front>
  <middle>
    <?line 21?>

<section anchor="introduction">
      <name>Introduction</name>
      <t>The key words "<bcp14>MUST</bcp14>", "<bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL
NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>RECOMMENDED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>NOT RECOMMENDED</bcp14>",
"<bcp14>MAY</bcp14>", and "<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>" in this document are to be interpreted as
described in BCP 14 <xref target="RFC2119"/> <xref target="RFC8174"/> when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.</t>
      <?line -18?>

<t>Nish has no public hex codec, and 37 test programs define their own. This spec
adds one, for tests, test vectors and configuration that carries keys in hex.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="interface">
      <name>Interface</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-interface"><![CDATA[
// module: nish/hex
export const hexEncode = (bytes: u8[]): string
export const hexDecode = (text: string): u8[] | null
]]></sourcecode>
      <t>A string is the bytes of its UTF-8 encoding, and every length and character in
this spec is counted in those bytes: a non-ASCII character is two to four
bytes, none of which is a hex digit.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="encoding">
      <name>Encoding</name>
      <section anchor="length">
        <name>Length</name>
        <t anchor="R-ENC-LEN">hexEncode <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> return exactly two characters per input byte.</t>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-ENC-LEN covers R-ENC-LEN — two characters per byte
// samples: 96
forall (b: u8[] /* 0..64 */) => hexEncode(b).length === 2 * b.length
]]></sourcecode>
      </section>
      <section anchor="digits">
        <name>Digits</name>
        <t anchor="R-ENC-DIGITS">Each byte <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> be written high nibble first, using the digits 0-9 and a-f.</t>
        <t anchor="R-ENC-ONLY">hexEncode <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> emit uppercase digits, separators or a prefix.</t>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-ENC-LOWER covers R-ENC-ONLY — the encoding has no uppercase digit
// samples: 96
forall (b: u8[] /* 0..64 */) => {
  const e = hexEncode(b);
  return e === e.toLowerCase();
}
]]></sourcecode>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-ROUND-TRIP covers R-ROUND-TRIP, R-ENC-ONLY — hexDecode(hexEncode(b)) is b
// samples: 96
forall (b: u8[] /* 0..64 */) => {
  const d = hexDecode(hexEncode(b));
  return d !== null && unwrap(d) === b;
}
]]></sourcecode>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section anchor="decoding">
      <name>Decoding</name>
      <section anchor="accepted-input">
        <name>Accepted input</name>
        <t anchor="R-DEC-ACCEPT">hexDecode <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> accept the digits 0-9, a-f and A-F, in any mix of case.</t>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-CANONICAL covers R-ENC-DIGITS, R-DEC-ACCEPT — lowercase hex of even length re-encodes to itself
// samples: 96
forall (t: string /* 0..32 hexdigit */) => {
  const d = hexDecode(t);
  return d !== null && hexEncode(unwrap(d)) === t;
}
where t.length % 2 === 0
]]></sourcecode>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-ANY-CASE covers R-DEC-ACCEPT — any mix of case decodes as its lowercase does
// samples: 96
forall (t: string /* 0..32 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */) => {
  const d = hexDecode(t);
  return d !== null && hexEncode(unwrap(d)) === t.toLowerCase();
}
where t.length % 2 === 0
]]></sourcecode>
      </section>
      <section anchor="rejected-input">
        <name>Rejected input</name>
        <t anchor="R-DEC-REJECT">hexDecode <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> return null when the text has an odd length or contains any
other character, including whitespace and a "0x" prefix.</t>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-ODD covers R-DEC-REJECT — an odd length is null
// samples: 96
forall (t: string /* 1..33 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */) => hexDecode(t) === null
where t.length % 2 === 1
]]></sourcecode>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-OTHER covers R-DEC-REJECT — any other printable character or whitespace, anywhere, is null
// samples: 96
forall (
  a: string /* 0..16 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */,
  x: string /* 1..1 " \t\n\r!\"#$%&'()*+,-./:;<=>?@GHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ[\\]^_`ghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz{|}~" */,
  z: string /* 0..16 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */,
) => hexDecode(a + (x + z)) === null
where (a + (x + z)).length % 2 === 0
]]></sourcecode>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-OTHER-EDGE covers R-DEC-REJECT — the characters next to each digit range are null
// samples: 96
forall (
  a: string /* 0..16 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */,
  x: string /* 1..1 "/:@G`g" */,
  z: string /* 0..16 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */,
) => hexDecode(a + (x + z)) === null
where (a + (x + z)).length % 2 === 0
]]></sourcecode>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-CONTROL covers R-DEC-REJECT — a control byte or DEL, anywhere in an even-length text, is null
// samples: 96
forall (
  a: string /* 0..16 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */,
  i: i32 /* 0..127 */,
  z: string /* 0..16 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */,
) => hexDecode(a + (unwrap(ascii(le64(i).slice(0, 1))) + z)) === null
where (i < 32 || i === 127) && (a.length + z.length) % 2 === 1
]]></sourcecode>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-PREFIX covers R-DEC-REJECT — a "0x" prefix is null
// samples: 96
forall (t: string /* 0..32 hexdigit HEXDIGIT */) => hexDecode("0x" + t) === null
where t.length % 2 === 0
]]></sourcecode>
      </section>
      <section anchor="empty-input">
        <name>Empty input</name>
        <t anchor="R-DEC-EMPTY">hexDecode of the empty string <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> return an empty array, not null.</t>
      </section>
      <section anchor="round-trip">
        <name>Round trip</name>
        <t anchor="R-ROUND-TRIP">For all bytes b, hexDecode(hexEncode(b)) <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> equal b.</t>
      </section>
      <section anchor="size">
        <name>Size</name>
        <t>This spec does not cover an input whose text would be longer than a string can
be, 2^31 - 1 bytes: hexEncode panics on 2^30 bytes or more, and hexDecode
answers null for a text of 2^31 - 1 bytes or more.</t>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section anchor="security-considerations">
      <name>Security Considerations</name>
      <t>Test vectors and configuration this module reads and writes are often key
material.</t>
      <t>hexEncode and hexDecode <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> branch on, or index memory by, the value of a
byte of their input, so that their running time depends only on its length.</t>
      <t>Untested: How the code runs, not what it returns: no functional test observes a branch, an address or a running time. Checked in nish instead: tests/cases/ct_asm_hex.ts holds the per-byte code (the digit of a nibble, a digit's validity and value, one byte encoded, one pair decoded) to no branch, no call and no secret-addressed access after clang -O2 on x86-64 and aarch64, and tests/link/std_hex_ct_copies holds those copies to the module. The loops themselves branch on their counter, which that check refuses outright, so their -O3 listings were read by hand.</t>
    </section>
